    "turn_text": "Well, you want me to talk about why it became important to me? Yeah. Yeah. And I probably actually someday like to do a whole podcast episode just on this experience in my life and just on self-care. Yeah, we got some interesting background noise. It's alright, you can deal with it. It's a beautiful May Day and someone is outside with their radio on. Gosh, it was eight years ago this summer that we moved to Utah and the period leading up to that was super stressful. I had four kids, my youngest was two, And we were leaving LA, which is where my family, my husband's family lived. And my husband had been practicing medicine with his father there. And we were very tight with his family, which was good. Because four years before that, we left my family in the Midwest. But we moved to Utah, and we had no family support at all, which was already stressful enough for me. Because when you make a move like that, and you totally lose your identity, and nobody knows who you are, where you came from, nothing, that was really, really tough for me. And on top of it, the summer that we moved, I was preparing a series of nine one-hour classes for Education Week up at BYU, Idaho, and that all happened literally. We didn't know when I was preparing and I signed up for it, but it all happened the same week that we moved. So we moved. We were staying in a condo waiting for our house to open up, and I was going up for the first time to teach these nine one-hour classes and that was a really going out on a limb for me. I had a lot of insecurity about that and it was a ton of preparation. That was super stressful and then of course that process of trying to get your kids settled and being in an unfamiliar place without any family support or anything and then that winter I found out that my sister back in the Midwest was most likely going to die from cancer. and long story short, it was on a Valentine's Day dinner with my husband talking about my sister that I had a full-blown panic attack that I thought was a heart attack and I ended up in the hospital for like two days",
